 "stimulus_html": "Use  Graph <i>L</i> to find a Hamilton path between each pair of vertices or indicate that there isn’t one.\n<figure>\n<figcaption>Graph <i>L</i></figcaption>\n<img src=\"exercises/media/55d5bfcc78afd582.png\" alt=\"Graph L has two quadrilaterals. The vertices of the first quadrilateral are m, o, n, and p. The vertices of the second quadrilateral are q, r, s, and t. Other edges connect m to n, n to q, and q to s.\">\n</figure>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 544519,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<i>p</i> to <i>r</i>",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>A path that visits each vertex exactly once is a Hamilton path. Recall that a path is a walk that has no repeated vertices or edges. It does not need to start and end on the same vertex.</p>\n<p>To find a bridge, avoid the bridges until you must cross them. This graph has a bridge (<i>nq</i>), so stay as far away from it as you can.</p>\n<p>Since you start at <i>p</i>, avoid being near the bridge and choose <i>m</i>. So far you have:</p>\n<p> <i>p → m</i></p>\n<p>Since you are staying away from the bridge, go to <i>o</i>.</p>\n<p><i>p → m → o</i></p><i>\n</i><p><i></i>Time to go over the bridge.</p>\n<p><i>p → m → o → n → q</i></p>\n<p>You need to end at <i>r</i>, so go around the vertices to end at <i>r.</i></p>\n<p><i>p → m → o → n → q → t → s → r</i></p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<i>p</i> → <i>m</i> → <i>o</i> → <i>n</i> → <i>q</i> → <i>t</i> → <i>s</i> → <i>r</i>"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
